  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery early this afternoon shows the local area in \nbetween multiple systems located in or around North America. One \nclosed low is moving through the Northeast into New England, one \nshortwave is moving into southern Manitoba, one low is well off the \ncoast of British Columbia, and another is cut off over the US-Mexico \nborder near AZ/NM. This leaves us with weak flow overall. At the \nsurface, high pressure is slow to shift east and holds fairly steady \nfor now over the Central Plains and mid-Mississippi River Valley, \nwhile a trough axis can be noted across the Dakotas into low \npressure in Canada. This system should work to push the sfc high \nsouth overnight tonight into Sunday. There may be some thin high \nclouds that move into northern KS after midnight, but this seems \nlike it would only have minor impacts on temperatures and fog \npotential, if any. It should be noted that the NBM is on the warm \nside of guidance for low temperatures Sunday morning, so with light \nwinds and only thin cirrus, it's possible crossover temperatures \ncould be reached sooner if we cool down faster and lows are cooler \nthan forecast. Soundings from the RRFS, RAP, and HRRR all show the \ninversion strengthening late tonight with shallow moisture at the \nsurface in the hours leading up to sunrise. Visibility probs from the\nREFS indicate fog to be patchy in nature with overall low chances \nfor dense fog at any particular location, but probs do get around or \nslightly above 50% briefly at a few spots. This would also point to \npatchy dense fog at times, similar to what occurred in north central \nKS this morning, except this could occur in low-lying spots anywhere \nin the forecast area.\n\nOnce any morning fog burns off, boundary layer mixing looks to be a \nlittle deeper Sunday compared to today, which should allow \ntemperatures to be warmer Sunday as well, along with abundant \nsunshine. Increasing mid-level heights with low-amplitude ridging \n(as the cut-off low stays south) favors warm temperatures continuing \nthrough the first part of next week. Above average highs are \nforecast Sunday through Wednesday, reaching at least the 60s through \nthat time with some 70s possible Monday and Tuesday. The cut-off low \nto our south weakens and becomes absorbed in the flow mid-week, \nbringing low rain chances southeast of our area into MO, but that's \nas close as we get to seeing any rain this upcoming week as it \nstands right now. \n\nUncertainty in the forecast increases later this week, particularly \nnext weekend. A shortwave dives southeast across the Upper Midwest \nWednesday and troughing deepens across roughly the eastern half of \nthe CONUS on Thursday, leading to an increasingly amplified pattern \nwith a ridge to our west, placing us in meridional flow aloft. \nEnsemble solutions vary on the westward extent of the trough, \nleading to uncertainty on whether we'll see more influence from the \ntrough or the ridge. The farther west the trough ends up, the \nstronger CAA we'll see behind the surface front. A more eastward \nsolution would keep the deeper cold air to our north and east. In \ngeneral, would expect a cooler trend late this week compared to the \nstart of the week, but 25th-75th percentile high temperature ranges \nare nearly 20 degrees by Friday and Saturday, so the extent of the \ncooldown is in flux. The current forecast lines up with a more \nmiddle ground solution in the 50s and 60s.",
